WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.150
- Today on Worth It, fries.

00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:03.460
Why don't you tell us what we're gonna do today

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:04.635
in a drastic kind of way?

00:00:04.635 --> 00:00:06.520
- (laughs) Today on Worth It, we're gonna be trying

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.120
three drastically awesome fries.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:09.510
At three drastically different price points,

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.000
to find out which fries are the most worth it at its price.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.810
- We're starting this episode here in Los Angeles.

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:16.853
And then later on, we're gonna be going international.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:18.750
- Oh, I love it.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:19.583
- We're going to--

00:00:19.583 --> 00:00:21.030
- [Together] Montreal!

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.410
- 'Cause we're doing french fries,

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.213
but we didn't wanna go to France,

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.470
(laughs) so we just went to French Canada.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:27.453
- Next best thing.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:28.750
- That's how that works, right?

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.737
- My name is Steven and I like to be even.

00:00:31.737 --> 00:00:33.600
The only thing that rhymes with Steven is even.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.900
You know what, that sucks.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.493
Unleavened bread.

00:00:36.557 --> 00:00:37.430
(laughter)

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:39.063
Let's go eat some fries.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:39.983
- That's you.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.357
Unleavened Steven.

00:00:42.277 --> 00:00:44.760
^(heavy guitar music)

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.010
^- Okay, I'm Steven Fretz.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.140
^I am the executive chef and co-owner

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:49.170
^of Top Round Roast Beef.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:50.060
- [Andrew] Tell us about Top Round.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:51.690
- [Fritz] We do roast beef sandwiches,

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.870
hand-cut french fries, we make our own ice cream custard.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.930
Our tag-line is "Chef-driven fast food."

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.150
- What does that mean for you here?

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.680
- We take the time to really focus on the technique.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.400
And everything in this kitchen is made in this kitchen.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.200
Our cheese whiz, our fries.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.540
There's a human element to everything we do.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.330
And that's the way that we want it.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:10.840
Today, we're gonna be trying our hand-cut curly fries.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.980
- [Andrew] How do you get to the curly fry shape?

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:14.980
I'm just realizing I've never seen

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:16.863
a potato turn into a curly fry.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.040
- It is a machine that was made for us.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.720
So, we're gonna put our potato in there.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:21.900
So, we're in there nice and tight.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.833
Do your hand like that and push hard.

00:01:24.461 --> 00:01:26.330
(machine whirring)

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:27.163
There you go!

00:01:27.163 --> 00:01:30.110
All of our frying in this kitchen is done in beef fat.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:32.420
- [Andrew] Beef fat is the classic way of doing it.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.310
- [Fritz] It all comes down to one thing, flavor.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:37.310
I think, as a chef, there's a lot more flavor

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.340
in beef fat than in everyday oil.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.440
We finish them with fine sea salt and serve 'em.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:42.470
- [Steven] And I see on every table

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:43.690
there's a seasoning shake.

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:45.680
- [Fritz] Yeah, it's the same exact seasoning

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:46.730
that we have on the beef.

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:47.900
It's kind of our signature.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.980
I recommend putting it on the fries.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.060
- What is the goal texture for a really great french fry?

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.920
- [Fritz] You wanna crispy fry on the outside,

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.520
and then you want it to be fluffy in the middle.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.560
That golden color is what you strive for.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.470
People need to understand that this is a live vegetable.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:04.800
Certain times of the year, you'll get a fry

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.650
that's more golden than a fry that's at the end

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.490
of the season that has more starch and will burn more.

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.030
You just have to be aware of the difference

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.843
in the potato and how it fries.

00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:15.072
(cash register dinging)

00:02:15.072 --> 00:02:17.290
- Oh man, what did you order for me Andrew?

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:19.820
^- You have a concrete and I have a strawberry shake.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:20.653
Cheers, Steven.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:21.583
- Cheers.

00:02:21.642 --> 00:02:22.970
(upbeat disco music)

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:23.970
- [Andrew] Oh, yeah.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:25.243
- Oh, that's really good.

00:02:25.243 --> 00:02:27.130
You should try this, it's real nice and milky.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.380
You know, like that whipped cream, that Oreo.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:30.293
- Okay, we're not here for,

00:02:30.293 --> 00:02:31.372
we're not doing an ice cream video today.

00:02:31.372 --> 00:02:32.205
- Okay, okay, okay.

00:02:32.205 --> 00:02:33.038
- We're doing--

00:02:33.038 --> 00:02:34.100
- [Together] Fries.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:35.950
- My new favorite thing on this show,

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:37.980
is when we just say the same thing at the same time.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:38.813
- Ready?

00:02:39.471 --> 00:02:41.000
Friendship.
- Potatoes!

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:41.833
- Okay.

00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:42.710
Look at how fun that is!

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:45.343
It looks like a shag carpet, but with potato.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:47.253
- Oh, nice.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.150
- It kinda looks like a cursive lower case A.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:50.983
- Cheers.

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:54.490
- Incredible.
- It's awesome.

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:55.660
It's surprisingly--

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:56.493
- Fluffy!

00:02:56.493 --> 00:02:57.326
- Yes!

00:02:57.326 --> 00:02:59.293
I was gonna say soft, but fluffy is the right word.

00:03:00.103 --> 00:03:02.463
- (laughs) Adam almost lunged in to eat my fry.

00:03:02.463 --> 00:03:03.623
You can have it.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:06.230
Should we try a little shake on it?

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:07.063
- Yes.

00:03:07.063 --> 00:03:07.896
I don't know if it's because I know that

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:10.793
it's flavored in beef fat, but it tastes meatier.

00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:12.643
Ooh, okay.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:14.063
- Delicious.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:17.220
Would it be obscene to have a fry in some ice cream?

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.373
Uh-oh, uh-oh, uh-oh, uh-oh.

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:20.580
- Oh no, don't lose it!

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.070
- That's really, very delicious.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:25.970
Strawberry ice cream on a beefy potato?

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:26.920
Highly recommended.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.060
- I was recently visiting my filmmaking friend,

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.100
and he was like, "To be ahead of the curve,

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.460
do what others won't."

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:35.210
And that is what they're doing in here with curly fries.

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:37.430
They're getting fresh potatoes from Idaho,

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:38.630
cooking it in beef fat--

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:40.010
- I like the way you say potato.

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:40.843
- Potato?

00:03:40.941 --> 00:03:42.652
- (laughs) Yeah.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:43.543
- How do you say it?

00:03:43.543 --> 00:03:44.376
- Potato.

00:03:44.376 --> 00:03:45.223
- Say potato.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:47.640
No, you're just saying it wrong.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:48.473
Potato.

00:03:48.473 --> 00:03:49.520
You have to enunciate.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.250
- You sound like in the Lord of the Rings.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:52.203
Potatoes!

00:03:52.203 --> 00:03:54.453
- (laughs) Can we try some of the roast beef?

00:03:54.583 --> 00:03:57.583
(upbeat funk music)

00:03:58.160 --> 00:03:59.740
- You wanna just go bite for bite on this?

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:00.573
- Go for it.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.880
- I'm sad we weren't eating this along with the fries.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:07.240
This is soft and delicious, this is crispy and delicious.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:08.243
This is smooth and delicious.

00:04:08.243 --> 00:04:09.620
- That is so...

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:10.720
- Oh yeah, let me have that back.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:12.720
- Yo, throw some of that on there, here.

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:14.410
Boom roasted.

00:04:14.503 --> 00:04:16.083
- You just, yeah, roast beef.

00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:17.043
- To Montreal.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:18.840
Which we're going to next.

00:04:19.513 --> 00:04:20.920
- Little old Canada.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:22.270
- We've traveled here because we've been

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:24.687
enticed by a certain dish that we're gonna

00:04:24.687 --> 00:04:26.130
get to later in this episode.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:27.150
But, before we get there--

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.530
- You might recall when we had our bagel episode,

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:32.660
we learned a little factoid about bagels in Canada.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:34.670
^- In Montreal, everything bagels

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:36.870
^are called all dressed bagels.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:40.470
- So, we decided to treat ourselves to a little

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:41.940
all dress action of our own.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:42.810
- [Steven] Oh, yeah.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:45.510
- I'll give one to this guy, I'll take one for myself.

00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:49.320
(humming)

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:51.563
(humming "The Star-Spangled Banner")

00:04:51.563 --> 00:04:52.733
Were you just singing the anthem?

00:04:52.733 --> 00:04:53.840
(laughter)

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:55.680
Where are we off to now?

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.823
- A little restaurant called LOV.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:04:59.143
- LOV?

00:04:59.143 --> 00:05:00.520
We're gonna try some vegan food.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:02.420
We had non-vegan french fries first.

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:05.000
The last place is definitely not gonna be vegan at all.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.873
But, this place we're doing kimchi fries.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.118
Let's try it.
- Ooh!

00:05:09.118 --> 00:05:11.430
^(relaxed jazz music)

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:13.660
^- I'm Stéphanie Audet, I'm the chef here

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:15.940
^at LOV restaurant in Montreal.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:17.260
- What kind of restaurant is LOV?

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:19.550
- We're specialized in botanical cuisine.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:22.520
So, we're serving vegetarian and vegan food.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:24.210
Today, I'm gonna make the kimchi fries,

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:25.300
the famous kimchi fries.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:26.993
- Was that your idea, first of all?

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:27.826
- Yeah!

00:05:27.826 --> 00:05:28.850
- Okay, and then where did it come from?

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.310
- I really love fermented foods in general.

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:34.390
It's really good for your gut, it's good for your system.

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:36.590
All the vegetables from the kimchis are grown in Québec.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:38.720
It's all organic and it's fermented here.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.930
I really wanted to place kimchi on the menu.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:42.380
I didn't want to put it somewhere

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:43.990
that it will scare customers so much.

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:45.470
So with the fries I was like,

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.340
everybody buys fries, it's super comfortable.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:50.313
And I think our clientele is a little bit

00:05:50.313 --> 00:05:51.390
(speaking in a foreign language).

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:52.465
Like everybody, so it's probably very new to them.

00:05:52.465 --> 00:05:55.948
- What was that expression you used?

00:05:55.948 --> 00:05:57.050
^(speaking in a foreign language)

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:58.670
- Mister, madame, everybody.

00:05:58.670 --> 00:05:59.503
- Oh.

00:05:59.503 --> 00:06:00.580
- (laughs) It sounds weird!

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:04.033
- Like Joe Schmo, Joe Schmo, anybody.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:05.820
- [Stéphanie] And then we add the kimchi to it.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.810
After that, we make the sesame mayo.

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:10.450
I thicken our house-made soy milk aioli

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:12.473
with a blend, in the Vitamix, of miso,

00:06:12.621 --> 00:06:16.470
sriracha, sugar, garlic, rice vinegar, sesame oil.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:19.060
Then we just squeeze it on top of the fried kimchi,

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:20.550
and then it's ready to eat.

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.570
- [Steven] What makes fries the perfect

00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:23.970
medium for that kimchi?

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.993
- Having this umami flavor to the fermented foods,

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:28.040
it's a good compliment.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:28.873
You wanna taste?

00:06:29.463 --> 00:06:30.296
- Yeah. (laughs)
- Definitely.

00:06:30.296 --> 00:06:31.129
- [Stéphanie] Sounds good, huh?

00:06:31.129 --> 00:06:31.962
(cash register dinging)

00:06:31.962 --> 00:06:33.720
- Such a cute cutting board.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:34.941
It's like a ping-pong paddle.

00:06:34.941 --> 00:06:35.774
(clicking tongue)

00:06:35.774 --> 00:06:37.697
You make a awesome ping-pong sound.

00:06:37.697 --> 00:06:40.120
- I love how every time we come to a restaurant,

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.440
we immediately gravitate towards something

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:45.610
that the restaurant does not care about whatsoever.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:46.460
- But, they do care about this

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:48.070
cucumber black pepper kombucha.

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:49.470
- Cucumber black pepper kombucha.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:51.070
- That's what I'm talking about.

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:54.103
- Ooh.

00:06:54.103 --> 00:06:57.220
Cucumber kombucha tastes like delicious pickle brine.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:59.630
- Yeah, if pickle brine tasted good.

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:00.990
Sorry for the pickle brine lovers out there.

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:03.253
- Okay, sorry, pickle brine does taste good.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:05.070
But, this is like a toned down,

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:06.500
beverage version of pickle brine.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:07.470
- Alright, let's dig in.

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:08.540
- Do you wanna go fry first?

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:10.040
- Fry first, always fry first.

00:07:10.985 --> 00:07:11.818
Cheers.

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:16.510
- Delicious fried potato.

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:17.750
- I got a little bit of mayo on mine.

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:19.417
- I got a little bit of mayo on mine.

00:07:19.417 --> 00:07:21.220
A little preview for the main feature here.

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:22.053
- Oh, yeah.

00:07:22.053 --> 00:07:23.070
- Now, I'm gonna go for a fork full.

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:25.680
'Cause I wanna get all these flavors in my mouth at once.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:26.883
Oh yeah, hold on.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:28.273
I'm making a mess.

00:07:28.273 --> 00:07:29.160
- [Steven] My turn, my turn.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:30.260
- [Andrew] Kim-cheers.

00:07:33.175 --> 00:07:35.110
Hell yeah, I could crush this for sure.

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:38.160
- This is not what you would think is vegan, first of all.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:39.660
- 'Cause usually when I'm eating kimchi,

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:41.350
it's with delicious meats.

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:43.980
- Yeah, this mayo, aioli, sesame thing,

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.970
it feels like I'm eating something very meaty.

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:48.750
And the potatoes are perfect vessels.

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:53.013
- For all the kimchi non-eaters, non-enjoyers out there.

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:55.040
You just need to understand that it is a pairing

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:57.927
for delicious, hot, savory food.

00:07:57.927 --> 00:07:58.760
- Yes.

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:00.223
- A hot, crisp french fry?

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:01.710
Kimchi is perfect with that.

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:04.163
- I don't feel like crap after eating these.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.000
Which is like, that's hard to say with fries.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.400
- And I've had cheese fries that taste less creamy

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:11.643
and delicious than these kimchi fries do.

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:13.240
- I thought that was cheese at the beginning.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:15.294
Hey chef, how do you say that saying again?

00:08:15.294 --> 00:08:19.377
^(speaking in a foreign language)

00:08:23.108 --> 00:08:24.108
- Who did it better?

00:08:26.396 --> 00:08:27.229
^No!

00:08:27.693 --> 00:08:30.270
- Let the record show, the chef pointed at me.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:33.873
- Every time we do this in any language, he beats me.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:34.833
I don't understand.

00:08:34.833 --> 00:08:36.452
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:08:36.452 --> 00:08:37.331
Okay, that one sounded very good.

00:08:37.331 --> 00:08:38.623
You sound like you're from Ratatouille.

00:08:38.873 --> 00:08:39.965
(laughter)

00:08:39.965 --> 00:08:43.048
(upbeat piano music)

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.610
(cash register dinging)

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:49.443
Fry fact!

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:51.820
^Thomas Jefferson is actually credited for bringing

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:53.553
^the french fry to the states.

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:54.663
^- Really?

00:08:54.663 --> 00:08:56.210
^He was serving as the American Minister

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:57.800
^to France in the 1780s.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.110
^And he was like, "This is delicious, let's bring that over."

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:02.410
He referred to them, I can't speak French, but

00:09:02.465 --> 00:09:04.150
(speaking in a foreign language).

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:05.530
- We're just gonna bleep that out.

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:07.780
And we'll get a actual French speaking person.

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:08.613
Yes!

00:09:08.613 --> 00:09:11.096
(woman speaking in a foreign language)

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:14.630
^Or potatoes deep fried while raw in small cuttings.

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:17.100
^- Just adding up to his list of accolades.

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:20.320
^Big on independence, big on writing, big on declaring.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:22.600
^- He brought the french fry over and we thank you, sir.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.420
So you may be wondering, how did we come up

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:25.920
with $100 dollar french fries?

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:28.343
Well, we're getting 15 pounds of poutine.

00:09:28.343 --> 00:09:29.933
- More like Munch-real.

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:31.723
'Cause we're munching.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:33.350
- Mm, not your best work.

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:34.183
- Sorry.

00:09:34.183 --> 00:09:35.016
- You ready?

00:09:35.016 --> 00:09:35.849
- Yeah.

00:09:35.849 --> 00:09:37.623
I mean, not at all, but sure!

00:09:37.714 --> 00:09:40.547
(soft jazz music)

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:43.730
^- Hi, I'm Costa, co-owner of Poutineville in Montreal.

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:45.450
^We prepared a nice meal for you today,

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:46.522
^it's called the Heart Attack.

00:09:46.522 --> 00:09:47.355
(laughter)

00:09:47.355 --> 00:09:48.680
- A nice meal called the Heart Attack.

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:49.513
- It's what we're known for.

00:09:49.513 --> 00:09:51.870
We get groups of two that come and challenge themselves

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:53.550
to who can eat the most out of the Heart Attack.

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:56.230
Seven years that we been open, only one man has done it.

00:09:56.230 --> 00:09:57.063
- One man?

00:09:57.063 --> 00:09:58.670
- And it took him 22 minutes to do it.

00:09:58.670 --> 00:09:59.700
- That is unbelievable.

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:00.790
So tell us about Poutineville,

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:02.190
what kind of establishment is this?

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:04.220
- Instead of us creating our own menu.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:06.100
We let the clients create their own poutines.

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:07.900
We have endless amount of toppings.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:10.010
Whether you wanna be vegan, whether you want a meat lovers.

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:12.360
We focus on great ingredients, fresh product,

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:14.290
and we let the customers get very creative.

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:15.123
- What is poutine?

00:10:15.123 --> 00:10:15.956
- Oh, wow.

00:10:15.956 --> 00:10:18.800
Poutine is funky fast food with a twist.

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:21.350
Fries loaded with lots of cheese and lots of gravy.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.290
What you put on top, is what makes you

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:24.640
different from everybody else.

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:26.260
- Growing up, you ate poutine a lot?

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:27.530
- That's where Poutineville actually started.

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:29.996
Whatever my mom made for dinner, the next day was a poutine.

00:10:29.996 --> 00:10:30.829
- [Andrew and Steven] Oh.

00:10:30.829 --> 00:10:33.070
- Whether it was lamb, spaghetti, beef.

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:34.910
It turned out to a poutine of the week.

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:36.273
The Heart Attack is a monster poutine.

00:10:36.273 --> 00:10:37.910
It's the biggest one in Montreal.

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:39.870
10 vegetables, chicken, bacon,

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:42.450
ground beef, hot dogs, sausages, steak.

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:45.030
A traditional poutine is made with a cheddar curd,

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:46.840
but we infuse it with mozzarella so we can get

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:48.320
a little bit more of the stringy effect.

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:50.900
So, when you pull the cheese out it's delicious.

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:52.520
It's a double layer poutine.

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:54.250
What we do, is we put a layer of potato,

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:56.170
half the ingredients, and a layer of cheese,

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:58.290
and then we build another poutine on top of that one,

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:00.870
which becomes a monstrosity, and we cover it

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:02.010
with gravy at the end.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:03.980
And as you can see, shortly, it's fantastic.

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:06.400
- [Andrew] Is this a fork and knife type of situation?

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:07.510
- [Costa] It's a fork and knife situation.

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:08.930
You should have a bib at the same time,

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:10.980
because it can become a little bit messy.

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:11.983
- Wrong clothing for this day.

00:11:11.983 --> 00:11:13.149
- [Costa] Wrong color for poutine.

00:11:13.149 --> 00:11:14.415
(cash register dinging)

00:11:14.415 --> 00:11:15.620
^- Can we take a second to just

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:17.640
^appreciate the majesty of this?

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:20.323
This is 15 pounds.

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:23.330
- When I was born, I was 10 pounds,

00:11:23.330 --> 00:11:24.250
and I thought that that was big.

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:25.210
- That is big.

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:26.060
- I'm sorry, Mom.

00:11:26.217 --> 00:11:27.050
- That is big for a baby.

00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:27.883
- I'm very sorry.

00:11:27.883 --> 00:11:28.716
- Cheers, Steven.

00:11:28.716 --> 00:11:29.549
- Let it commence!

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:33.433
- Potatoey delicious.

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:34.283
- Solid.

00:11:34.283 --> 00:11:36.670
That fry is exactly what I wanted in these fries.

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:38.800
A good base, starchy but ready

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:40.830
to just soak in the other juices.

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:42.500
I'm gonna roll up my sleeves.

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:44.713
This is definitely a sleeve rolling dish.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:47.043
May the best competitor win.

00:11:47.447 --> 00:11:50.530
(upbeat salsa music)

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:52.920
- Up until now, I wasn't convinced

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:54.360
that gravy belonged on fries.

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:56.163
- Oh, gravy belongs on everything.

00:11:56.229 --> 00:11:57.062
What are you talking about?

00:11:57.062 --> 00:11:59.040
I have three different meats in one go.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:01.090
- You don't even have a fry on your fork.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:02.408
- Don't need it.

00:12:02.408 --> 00:12:03.723
- Look at this thing.

00:12:03.723 --> 00:12:06.683
I feel like a giant that's just eating the earth.

00:12:06.695 --> 00:12:08.430
- I feel like a mouse trying to eat human food.

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:10.972
- I think that's just how our personal outlooks on life are.

00:12:10.972 --> 00:12:13.210
- (laughs) You're digging quite a hole there.

00:12:13.210 --> 00:12:14.867
- I've reached the second layer of cheese.

00:12:14.867 --> 00:12:17.090
It's kind of like how you can tell

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:19.510
how old a fossil is based on what other

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:21.030
materials have laid on top of it.

00:12:21.030 --> 00:12:23.970
There's no way a human ate this in 22 minutes.

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:24.803
- [Steven] There's footage!

00:12:24.803 --> 00:12:25.636
I watched it just now.

00:12:25.636 --> 00:12:26.970
- So, I'm gonna make the claim that he's not human.

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:29.120
We need to turn this into an Unsolved episode.

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:31.433
- Oh. (singing)

00:12:31.433 --> 00:12:32.570
Wait, what is their music?

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:33.403
- Wah!

00:12:33.512 --> 00:12:34.345
(laughter)

00:12:34.345 --> 00:12:38.540
A man claimed to have eaten 15 pounds of poutine,

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:42.270
but how could he do it in 22 minutes?

00:12:42.270 --> 00:12:43.103
- Ghosts!

00:12:43.103 --> 00:12:45.100
- And then Shane goes, "Well, don't say he's an alien!"

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:46.050
- What's happening?

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:47.370
- I think we got the meat delirium.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:50.030
You make a dent and then it caves in on itself.

00:12:50.030 --> 00:12:51.200
So, do you like poutine?

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:52.520
- I'm really loving this, yeah.

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:55.050
The great thing about it is, you never get bored.

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:58.270
You can just make your own poutine in this poutine.

00:12:58.270 --> 00:12:59.920
Right now, I'm feeling really meaty.

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:01.470
But then later I'm like, "Ah, too much bacon."

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:02.670
So, I go more fries.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:05.080
Whatever you wanna do, you can just assemble that.

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:07.610
Literally, in this massive plate of poutine.

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:09.320
- I think you're winning right now.

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:10.153
- [Steven] Oh, yeah.

00:13:10.153 --> 00:13:11.880
So, if you actually wanted to finish this,

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:13.040
how many people would you bring?

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:13.873
17 people?

00:13:13.873 --> 00:13:15.113
- I don't know I can't count.

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:18.453
- Oh, yeah, we've barely touched it.

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:20.143
Oh, do I see the bottom yet?

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:21.813
No.

00:13:21.831 --> 00:13:22.664
(laughter)

00:13:22.664 --> 00:13:23.663
- It's been 20 minutes.

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:26.460
In the time that we finished two small dents of food,

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:28.983
a man came in and ate the entire thing.

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:31.083
- That seems impossible for a human.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:32.753
- I wanna just take a nap.

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:36.223
Oh, man.

00:13:37.158 --> 00:13:38.580
- Can I feed you one final bite?

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:39.466
- No, I can't.

00:13:39.466 --> 00:13:40.299
- Why not?

00:13:40.299 --> 00:13:41.310
- Please put the curds away.

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:46.213
- That's why it's called poutine, you put in.

00:13:46.466 --> 00:13:48.716
(laughter)

00:13:48.924 --> 00:13:52.674
(dark slow electronic music)

00:13:53.475 --> 00:13:55.361
(upbeat funk music)

00:13:55.361 --> 00:13:57.400
- I'm just trying to work off the fries, okay, I just--

00:13:57.400 --> 00:13:58.700
- You can try, but you won't.

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:00.710
Those are now part of your body permanently.

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:01.543
- No!

00:14:01.543 --> 00:14:04.400
Which fry at which price point was most worth it to you?

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:07.393
^- Those fries at Poutineville could feed an army.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:08.880
^- That was insane value.

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:10.560
15 pounds, $100.

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:13.550
- If I was in high school and on the football team,

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:15.030
that is the food that I would wanna eat.

00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:16.770
But, my Worth It winner goes to Top Round.

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:19.160
The fact that you got a little curly action on that?

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:19.993
That's just fun.

00:14:19.993 --> 00:14:21.900
Fries are about fun, and the curly fry

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:23.070
is the funnest fry of them all.

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:25.730
- My Worth It winner goes to the kimchi fries at LOV.

00:14:25.730 --> 00:14:26.840
It was just delicious.

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:28.440
I felt great after I ate them.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:30.240
^- Adam, what's your Worth It winner?

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:33.020
So, that ends things for us here in Montreal.

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:34.503
It was delightful trip.

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:36.301
- [Steven] You know who needs to go to Montreal?

00:14:36.301 --> 00:14:37.760
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:38.593
Ah, shoot.

00:14:38.835 --> 00:14:39.814
(Andrew speaking in a foreign language)

00:14:39.814 --> 00:14:40.760
(Steven speaking in a foreign language)

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:42.260
- [Andrew] You only said that, because I said it first.

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:43.880
So edit that, where I don't say it.

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:44.805
- [Stephen] No, no!

00:14:44.805 --> 00:14:46.086
- He couldn't remember that.

00:14:46.086 --> 00:14:47.340
He also couldn't remember how to say it either.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:49.410
Hey, check out our Instagram, it's new.

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:50.800
It's @buzzfeedworthit.

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:52.450
- See you guys there.

00:14:52.450 --> 00:14:53.753
Oh, yes!

